**Question 1 of 3**

<ask type="short">In card processing, what does clearing produce that settlement then acts on?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
the amounts each side owes for the day's transactions. clearing works out the positions, settlement is the money actually moving
@@ tutor
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"clearing works out the positions, settlement is the money actually moving" is the distinction: clearing exchanges the transaction records and computes what each party owes, and settlement pays those amounts.</grade>

**Question 2 of 3**

An acquirer's clearing file for one day shows card sales of £18,400.00 and refunds of £650.00. Interchange due to issuers on that day's activity is £52.00. Ignoring scheme fees, what net amount does the acquirer receive at settlement?

<solution>18,400.00 − 650.00 − 52.00 = £17,698.00. Refunds flow back to issuers, and interchange is kept by issuers out of what they send.</solution>
<check lang="python">
assert 18400 - 650 - 52 == 17698
</check>

<ask type="problem">The net amount, with your working.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
18400 - 650 = 17750. plus 52 interchange = 17802
@@ tutor
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.4">"18400 - 650 = 17750" is right. "plus 52 interchange" has the direction reversed: interchange is paid by the acquiring side to the issuers, so it is deducted, and the acquirer receives 17,750.00 − 52.00 = £17,698.00.</grade>

<card>At settlement, in which direction does interchange flow? :: From the acquirer's side to the issuer. The issuer keeps it out of the sale amount it sends, so it reduces what the acquirer receives.</card>

**Question 3 of 3**

<ask type="choice" options="The issuer, which deducts it from what it sends the acquirer|The acquirer, which adds it to what it pays the merchant|The network, which keeps it as its fee for the routing|The merchant, which receives it as a rebate on the sale">On a £100.00 card sale with £0.30 of interchange, who ends up holding the £0.30?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
The issuer, which deducts it from what it sends the acquirer. so the acquirer only gets 99.70
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"so the acquirer only gets 99.70" is right, and it is the direction you had reversed in the last question: the issuer keeps the £0.30 and sends on £99.70.</grade>

<summary>**Score:** 2 of 3 correct, with the other partly right.

**Strengths:** the split between clearing (working out the positions) and settlement (paying them), and netting refunds against sales.

**Misconception to fix:** the direction of interchange. In Question 2 you added the £52.00 and got £17,802.00; it is deducted, giving £17,698.00. You had it right in Question 3, on a single sale.

**Review:** the flow of funds on one sale from cardholder to merchant, then a full day's settlement with scheme fees included.</summary>
@@ verify
from decimal import Decimal as D
assert 18400 - 650 == 17750 and 17750 + 52 == 17802 and 17750 - 52 == 17698
assert D('100.00') - D('0.30') == D('99.70')
# marks in the history: correct, partial, correct
import re
marks = re.findall(r'verdict="(\w+)"', tutor[1] + tutor[2] + reply)
assert marks == ['correct', 'partial', 'correct']
assert '17,698.00' in solution(tutor[1])
